\import{algorithms-macros}
\title{Proving \GCDR correct}
\date{2026-09-02T13:42:38Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\p{We will begin by formally proving the correctness of [the recursive
Euclidean Algorithm](00K4). But before we can even do that, we have to
back up a bit: what does it even mean for an algorithm to be
"correct"?}

\transclude{00K6}  % specification vs implementation

\p{In this case, the \em{specification} we have in mind is the mathematical definition of #{\gcd}; the implementation we have in mind is the \sf{GCDR} function.  We are starting with \sf{GCDR} because it's usually much easier to prove things about recursive algorithms than about iterative ones!}

\p{In order to prove that \sf{GCDR} is correct, we first need two small lemmas about the mathematical #{\gcd} function.  The proofs are included only for completeness—feel free to skip them unless you are interested.}

\transclude{00F3}
\transclude{00F8}

\p{Now we can prove that \sf{GCDR} matches its specification.}

